\subsection{Motivation}

\subsubsection{Graph Signal Recovery}
\paragraph{Background} Graph signals, defined as scalar values assigned to graph nodes, have garnered significant research interest due to their relevance in diverse fields such as machine learning, control theory, and signal processing. Consequently, developing efficient reconstruction algorithms is crucial. Recent years have seen extensive literature on graph signal recovery, particularly through regularization techniques that regularize the graph Laplacian norm to promote smoothness. These methods leverage the inherent structure within graph signals to mitigate the negative impacts of noise.

\paragraph{Current Status} Existing recovery methods generally fall into two categories: those based on $\ell_1$-norm regularization and those relying on correntropy. The $\ell_1$-norm approach offers robustness against certain noise types but fails to adapt to others. Conversely, correntropy-based methods provide adaptability; however, ad-hoc selections of correlation functions often result in suboptimal performance. To address this limitation, this paper proposes adapting the correlation function itself to further enhance performance.

\paragraph{Contribution} This work makes three primary contributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We generalize adaptive methods to graph signal recovery by considering the cost function parameters of correntropy to derive an efficient solution, defining this as "generalized correntropy."
    \item A specific formulation of the correlation function is learned via optimization to prove that generalized correntropy provides superior performance compared to traditional correntropy.
    \item Theoretical analysis characterizing the algorithm's stability is provided.
\end{itemize}

